# Implementation plans must end with handoff notes

Every implementation plan (e.g. under `docs/superpowers/plans/`) MUST end with a
section titled:

```
## Handoff notes / gotchas (read before starting)
```

A fresh agent in a new session executes the plan with **zero** memory of the
conversation that produced it. Scan the whole conversation and distill the
tacit context that isn't already in the codebase or the task steps. Keep it to
tight, concrete bullets — only what a fresh agent would get wrong or waste time
rediscovering:

- **Prior/related work this builds on** — existing modules, commits, files to read first (so it extends, not re-creates).
- **Bugs already hit + traps to avoid** — ordering requirements, version/port quirks, platform gotchas surfaced in the conversation.
- **Environment specifics** not obvious from code — ports, hostnames, OS quirks, resource limits (e.g. small VM), required service versions.
- **Non-obvious constraints/decisions and why** — e.g. "default-off, best-effort, never block the request path"; "no new container due to RAM limit".
- **Secrets / files not to touch or commit** — e.g. gitignored env files with real credentials; edit the `.example` instead.
- **Tooling/hook behavior** the agent will hit — pre-edit gates, pre-commit/pre-push hooks.
- **How success is verified** if non-obvious — visual check in a specific UI; expected latencies so slowness isn't mistaken for a bug.

If the conversation surfaced no such context, still include the section with the
load-bearing facts (prior work + how to verify).
